WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.589
Hey David, what is worse after what happened
in Iowa this last week?

00:00:05.589 --> 00:00:09.910
The electoral college or the caucus system,
which is more broken?

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:10.910
Well.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:14.820
What's interesting about the electoral college
system and the caucus system is that aside

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:18.820
from the ways in which they leave themselves
open to manipulation, that's not what I'm

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:20.400
going to deal with today.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.140
They're kind of broken in the same way.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.140
I mean one.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:29.619
So with Iowa, the complaints came around,
you know, the app that was used, the reporting,

00:00:29.619 --> 00:00:32.660
the sketchy way in which, how wasn't it tested
fully?

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:34.010
Like we get all that stuff.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:38.481
But really when it comes down to it, Bernie
Sanders won the popular vote in Iowa, but

00:00:38.481 --> 00:00:40.980
did not get the most number of delegates.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:46.960
That is a similar problem to winning the popular
vote nationally and not winning the electoral

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:47.960
college.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.360
Hillary Clinton winning by several million
votes and Donald Trump's still winning the

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:51.670
electoral college.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:53.210
The problems are very similar.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:56.170
They're broken in very a similar ways.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:04.470
Now you can argue that the caucus system is
less obviously a disaster at a state level

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:09.570
because you can conceivably still handle an
election at the state level under that caucus

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:13.900
system, which would just be absolute and total
chaos nationally.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.280
But that's not what we're comparing.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.670
We're comparing caucuses and States and the
electoral college system nationally.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:26.520
I think that the electoral college system
has had more of an impact in elections.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.880
If you look at the number now of Republican
presidents who have won electorally but lost

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:37.380
the popular vote, that is a system that has
handed numerous presidencies to the Republican

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:38.380
party.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.840
When you look at the caucus system in States,
there's no question that between the coin

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:49.381
flips and winning the popular vote, but losing
the delegate count at the state level, there

00:01:49.381 --> 00:01:56.150
have been a effects from having the caucus
system, but as far as I was able to research

00:01:56.150 --> 00:02:00.040
going back about 30 years, the caucus system
has not flipped.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.720
Who ultimately was the nominee in the democratic
party.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.400
Super delegates had been a big part of that.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.830
That's separate from the caucus system.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:09.530
You can look at that as something that has
actually changed the outcome of who is the

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:15.520
democratic nominee, but the caucus system
has not flipped the results in the way that

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:21.250
the electoral system, nationally has giving
the presidency to multiple Republicans who

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:22.850
actually lost the popular vote.

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:27.860
So if you asked me, which has been worse in
terms of its impact, I have to say that it's

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:31.319
the electoral system changing either or.

00:02:31.319 --> 00:02:36.670
Both has its quirks, of course, States often,
uh, although debt, although the democratic

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:40.959
and Republican parties are not government
institutions and they can run their primary

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:47.270
however they want, States often have laws
about when their primary is or how it works.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:50.320
So New Hampshire has to be the first primary.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.830
Iowa has to be the first to vote at least
eight days before any primary, which is why

00:02:55.830 --> 00:03:01.410
February 3rd was the day of the Iowa caucus,
and eight days later, February 11th is the

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:04.200
day a was the day of the New Hampshire primary.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:10.050
So you can change that at the state level
to get rid of the electoral college system.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:15.030
Of course, you would either need a constitutional
amendment or it could happen to facto through

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:21.650
something called the NPV icy national popular
vote interstate compact States can decide

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:27.670
unilaterally how to award their electoral
votes and a state, for example, Texas could

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:31.180
say, we don't care which candidate wins Texas.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:35.940
We're going to give our delegates to the winner
of the national popular vote.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:42.430
If you get enough States to do that, that
they some 270 electoral votes, you create

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:50.010
a de facto national popular vote that seems
by far the most viable and likely way we could

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.629
get a national popular vote.

00:03:51.629 --> 00:03:55.709
You are not going to see, you know when you've
got to have a two thirds vote in the Senate

00:03:55.709 --> 00:04:00.440
and three quarters of the States say, let's
get rid of the electoral college.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:05.310
All of the red States that know that they've
had multiple presidencies handed to them by

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.510
the electoral college, they're not going to
do it.

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:12.470
So something like the NPV IC would really
be the only likely way that I see of getting

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.989
rid of the electoral college and switching
to a national popular vote.

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:17.030
Let me know what you think.

